    "summary": "High‑energy Spectrum show with soaring Let It Grow jam and tight horn‑rich medley",
    "blurb": "The band delivers a blistering first set highlighted by Big Railroad Blues, Black Peter and a driving Playing In The Band. The second half shines with an expansive Let It Grow jam and a seamless He's Gone > Truckin' > The Other One > Wharf Rat sequence, capped off by a horn‑filled Casey Jones encore. Overall the performance is consistently high‑energy and well‑executed.",
    "review": "September 21, 1973 at The Spectrum finds the Dead firing on all cylinders. Jerry Garcia’s crystal‑clear tone rides over every groove, from his soaring leads in Let It Grow to a standout solo on Row Jimmy that lifts the horn‑rich second set. Phil Lesh anchors the marathon medley with a bass line on The Other One that erupts out of nowhere, while Bob Weir’s steady rhythm guitar keeps the band locked together. Keith Godchaux adds lively, textural keyboards that weave through the horns, and Bill Kreutzmann drives the high‑energy jams with crisp snare/kick work; Mickey Hart stays mostly in the background but peppers the set with subtle accents. Highlights include a blistering Big Railroad Blues opener, an immaculate Black Peter, the expansive Let It Grow jam enhanced by the horn section, and the flawless He's Gone > Truckin' > The Other One > Wharf Rat sequence that rolls into Row Jimmy before a rousing Casey Jones encore. The matrix recording captures both board clarity and audience ambience, making this one of the most vivid snapshots of the 1973 era.",
